## Environment
- OS Version: Ubuntu 24.04
- Source or binary build? gz-sim main (e08ab4e) built from source; gz-physics dartsim plugin from the nightly packages of 2026-09-12 (gz-physics b6dbf127); DART 6.16.6 and Bullet 3.24 from the Ubuntu/OSRF packages
- Rendering plugin: none (headless server)

## Description
- Expected behavior: small boxes dropped on a flat heightmap come to rest on its surface, as they do with the default (ode) collision detector.
- Actual behavior: with `<collision_detector>bullet</collision_detector>`, 2 cm boxes sink into the heightmap or fall through it, depending on where they land. 2 cm spheres and 4 cm boxes are not affected.

The cause appears to be in Bullet's contact generation between a `btBoxShape` with its default margin and a `btHeightfieldTerrainShape` with small cells, which DART uses for heightmaps. Details under Output.

## Steps to reproduce
1. Create a flat 129 x 129 heightmap image next to the world file:
   ```python
   import struct, zlib
   n = 129
   raw = b"".join(b"\0" + bytes([255] * n) for _ in range(n))
   chunk = lambda t, d: struct.pack(">I", len(d)) + t + d + struct.pack(">I", zlib.crc32(t + d) & 0xFFFFFFFF)
   open("flat.png", "wb").write(b"\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n" + chunk(b"IHDR", struct.pack(">IIBBBBB", n, n, 8, 0, 0, 0, 0))
                               + chunk(b"IDAT", zlib.compress(raw)) + chunk(b"IEND", b""))
   ```
2. Save this world as `heightmap_boxes.sdf`. The heightmap is a flat surface at z = 1 over 7 m x 7 m, so its cells are about 5.4 cm wide. Four 2 cm boxes are dropped from 10 cm above it.
   ```xml
   <?xml version="1.0"?>
   <sdf version="1.9">
     <world name="heightmap_boxes">
       <physics name="1ms" type="dart">
         <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
         <real_time_factor>1</real_time_factor>
         <dart><collision_detector>bullet</collision_detector></dart>
       </physics>
       <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
       <model name="ground">
         <static>true</static>
         <link name="link">
           <collision name="collision">
             <geometry>
               <heightmap>
                 <uri>flat.png</uri>
                 <size>7 7 1</size>
               </heightmap>
             </geometry>
           </collision>
         </link>
       </model>
       <model name="box_0">
         <pose>0 0 1.1 0 0 0</pose>
         <link name="link">
           <inertial>
             <mass>0.1</mass>
             <inertia><ixx>6.7e-6</ixx><iyy>6.7e-6</iyy><izz>6.7e-6</izz></inertia>
           </inertial>
           <collision name="collision">
             <geometry><box><size>0.02 0.02 0.02</size></box></geometry>
           </collision>
         </link>
       </model>
       <!-- box_1, box_2 and box_3: the same model at (3, 0), (0, -3) and (1.5, 0) -->
     </world>
   </sdf>
   ```
3. Run `gz sim -s -r heightmap_boxes.sdf` and after a few seconds read the boxes' heights with `gz model -m box_<n> -p`.
4. Change the collision detector to `ode` and repeat.

## Output
Height of each box's center after 5 s. A box resting on the surface is at z = 1.01.

| Box | Start (x, y) | bullet | ode |
|---|---|---|---|
| box_0 | (0, 0) | 1.0100 | 1.0100 |
| box_1 | (3, 0) | 1.0032 (sunk 6.8 mm) | 1.0100 |
| box_2 | (0, -3) | 0.9949 (sunk 15 mm, its center is below the surface) | 1.0100 |
| box_3 | (1.5, 0) | -50.2 (fell through) | 1.0100 |

The results repeat exactly from run to run. On a rough heightmap (257 x 257 pixels, 14 m x 7 m) the same happens to 2 cm boxes in about a third of the places tried. With 2 cm spheres, or with boxes of 4 cm or more, every body rests at the right height. The `fcl` detector doesn't support heightmaps at all: DART logs `Attempting to create an unsupported shape type [HeightmapShape (f)]. Creating a sphere with 0.1 radius instead.` and every body falls.

The problem reproduces in Bullet alone, without DART or Gazebo. The program below builds the heightfield the way DART's `BulletCollisionDetector` does (`btHeightfieldTerrainShape`, local scaling of size / pixels, zigzag subdivision, in a compound shifted to the minimum height), places a 2 cm box 1 mm into a flat heightfield at 64 positions across one cell, and checks the contact that `contactPairTest` reports:

| Cell size | Box margin | Wrong or missing contacts |
|---|---|---|
| 5.4 cm | Bullet default (1 mm for this box) | 54 of 64 |
| 5.4 cm | 0 | 0 of 64 |
| 1 m | Bullet default | 0 of 64 |
| 10 cm | Bullet default | 0 of 64 |
| 5.4 cm, 3 cm box | Bullet default | 0 of 64 |

In the failing placements `contactPairTest` returns no contact, or a contact with a positive distance and a tilted normal, for a box that overlaps the surface by 1 mm.

<details><summary>Bullet-only check (bt_hf.cc)</summary>

```cpp
// g++ -O2 -I/usr/include/bullet bt_hf.cc -lBulletCollision -lLinearMath
// usage: a.out <scale> <box half extent> [box margin]
#include <btBulletCollisionCommon.h>
#include <BulletCollision/CollisionShapes/btHeightfieldTerrainShape.h>
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <vector>

struct Cb : btCollisionWorld::ContactResultCallback {
  int n = 0; double minDepth = 1e9;
  btScalar addSingleResult(btManifoldPoint &cp, const btCollisionObjectWrapper *, int, int,
                           const btCollisionObjectWrapper *, int, int) override {
    ++n; minDepth = std::fmin(minDepth, cp.getDistance()); return 0;
  }
};

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  const int w = 129;
  const double scale = atof(argv[1]), half = atof(argv[2]), h = 0.0586, pen = 0.001;
  std::vector<float> data(w * w, float(h));
  btHeightfieldTerrainShape hf(w, w, data.data(), 1, h, h, 2, PHY_FLOAT, false);
  hf.setLocalScaling(btVector3(scale, scale, 1));
  hf.setUseZigzagSubdivision(true);
  btCompoundShape comp;
  comp.addChildShape(btTransform(btMatrix3x3::getIdentity(), btVector3(0, 0, h)), &hf);
  btDefaultCollisionConfiguration cfg; btCollisionDispatcher disp(&cfg); btDbvtBroadphase bp;
  btCollisionWorld world(&disp, &bp, &cfg);
  btCollisionObject ground; ground.setCollisionShape(&comp); world.addCollisionObject(&ground);
  btBoxShape box(btVector3(half, half, half));
  if (argc > 3) box.setMargin(atof(argv[3]));
  btCollisionObject obj; obj.setCollisionShape(&box); world.addCollisionObject(&obj);
  int bad = 0;
  for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k) {
    obj.setWorldTransform(btTransform(btMatrix3x3::getIdentity(),
        btVector3(0.3 + k * scale / 8, 0.3 + j * scale / 8, h + half - pen)));
    world.updateAabbs();
    Cb cb; world.contactPairTest(&obj, &ground, cb);
    if (!(cb.n > 0 && std::fabs(cb.minDepth + pen) < 2e-4)) ++bad;
  }
  printf("margin %g: %d of 64 placements wrong\n", box.getMargin(), bad);
}
```
`a.out 0.0543 0.01` prints `margin 0.001: 54 of 64 placements wrong`; `a.out 0.0543 0.01 0` prints `margin 0: 0 of 64 placements wrong`.
</details>

DART creates `btBoxShape` without setting a margin, so Bullet's default applies: `btBoxShape` lowers the 0.04 default to 10% of the smallest half extent, 1 mm for a 2 cm box. DART already sets a zero margin for cones and convex meshes in `BulletCollisionDetector.cpp`. Doing the same for boxes, here or in DART, would likely fix this case. The root cause is probably Bullet's convex-triangle contact generation with small triangles, so I'm happy to move this to dartsim/dart or to Bullet if that's a better place.

Separately, it would help if gz-physics warned that the `fcl` detector can't collide with heightmaps, instead of DART quietly substituting a 0.1 m sphere.
